1
00:00:28,230 --> 00:00:30,980
Legal documents face two quiet risks.

2
00:00:31,680 --> 00:00:36,930
First, a file can be changed after everyone agreed to it, and nobody notices.

3
00:00:37,630 --> 00:00:41,600
Second, anyone with a login can upload or approve from anywhere.

4
00:00:42,300 --> 00:00:45,370
GeoLegalVault is a prototype that tackles both.

5
00:00:46,070 --> 00:00:51,826
Uploads and approvals are allowed only from an approved area. That area is called a geofence,

6
00:00:51,826 --> 00:00:53,930
a virtual boundary drawn on a map.

7
00:00:54,630 --> 00:00:59,780
Every document is reviewed and approved by different people, so nobody approves their own work.

8
00:01:00,480 --> 00:01:05,728
When a version is approved, a fingerprint of the file, called a SHA-256 hash,

9
00:01:05,728 --> 00:01:09,000
is written to the Ethereum Sepolia test network.

10
00:01:09,700 --> 00:01:16,449
Later, anyone can recompute the fingerprint and see whether the file still matches. This is tamper-evident:

11
00:01:16,449 --> 00:01:18,720
changes are detected, not prevented.

12
00:01:19,420 --> 00:01:26,286
In about twenty minutes we follow one fresh document through its whole life: upload, approval, anchoring,

13
00:01:26,286 --> 00:01:29,490
tampering, an amendment, a download, and archive.

14
00:01:30,190 --> 00:01:36,677
Everything you will see is the real running system, on a test network. Where the screen cannot show something,

15
00:01:36,677 --> 00:01:39,920
like database records, I label it a supplementary view.

16
00:01:40,620 --> 00:01:47,309
First, login and roles. There are five roles: Administrator, Legal Officer, Reviewing Officer,

17
00:01:47,309 --> 00:01:51,650
Authorized Staff and Auditor. A role is a set of permissions.

18
00:01:52,370 --> 00:01:58,959
Here Authorized Staff is signed in. The welcome line shows the person's name, not a database id.

19
00:01:58,959 --> 00:02:02,940
The menu has Upload, but no Admin Panel and no Audit Logs.

20
00:02:03,640 --> 00:02:07,445
These counts come from the server, and they differ for each person.

21
00:02:07,445 --> 00:02:11,591
In total the vault holds twenty-one demo documents, two of them archived,

22
00:02:11,591 --> 00:02:14,260
and archived ones are left out of these counts.

23
00:02:14,990 --> 00:02:22,223
Next, a Legal Officer signs in, in a completely separate browser session. This role has a higher clearance,

24
00:02:22,223 --> 00:02:23,980
so it sees more documents.

25
00:02:24,680 --> 00:02:30,396
Then an Administrator. Their menu shows the Admin Panel and Audit Logs, but no Upload.

26
00:02:30,396 --> 00:02:35,780
Administrators manage users and zones, but they cannot upload, review or approve.

27
00:02:36,480 --> 00:02:42,996
Every person also has a clearance level: Public, Internal, Confidential, Restricted or Top Secret.

28
00:02:42,996 --> 00:02:49,180
The menu is only a convenience. The server checks role and clearance on every single request.

29
00:02:49,880 --> 00:02:55,380
Now the Administrator's Admin Panel. The Geofences tab lists the approved zones.

30
00:02:56,089 --> 00:03:01,191
A zone is a polygon: a list of longitude and latitude pairs, always in that order.

31
00:03:01,191 --> 00:03:08,097
The form previews the approximate centre of the ring you type, and asks the administrator to confirm the place.

32
00:03:08,097 --> 00:03:13,759
That preview is only a visual aid. Our demo zone, HQ Campus, is the small box listed below,

33
00:03:13,759 --> 00:03:18,861
around latitude eleven point six seven and longitude seventy-eight point one five.

34
00:03:18,861 --> 00:03:21,909
Every request is tested against it on the server.

35
00:03:22,609 --> 00:03:28,019
Now the Users tab. People are shown by name, with their role and clearance.

36
00:03:28,728 --> 00:03:34,984
We create a throwaway account with the Auditor role. A new user starts at the lowest clearance, Public,

37
00:03:34,984 --> 00:03:36,988
and the password field is masked.

38
00:03:37,692 --> 00:03:44,052
The new account appears in the table. We will use it later, to show a hidden document and the login protection.

39
00:03:44,768 --> 00:03:49,761
Now the main flow, with one fresh document. Authorized Staff opens Upload.

40
00:03:49,761 --> 00:03:56,170
The page shows the location the browser reports. On a real device that comes from GPS or Wi-Fi.

41
00:03:56,170 --> 00:04:02,848
In this recording a test harness supplies it, and the label says so. The server still validates it.

42
00:04:03,559 --> 00:04:09,749
We enter a title, the type Lease, the classification Internal, and pick a sample PDF.

43
00:04:10,454 --> 00:04:17,288
On Upload, the server checks, in a fixed order: your token, your role, your location, then the file.

44
00:04:17,288 --> 00:04:24,327
Then it stores the file, computes the hash and writes an audit entry. The document is saved as a Draft,

45
00:04:24,327 --> 00:04:26,104
owned by Authorized Staff.

46
00:04:26,820 --> 00:04:32,220
In this supplementary view, the real database shows the document row and the version row.

47
00:04:32,220 --> 00:04:38,712
The highlighted field is the SHA-256 fingerprint of the file. It turns any file into sixty-four characters,

48
00:04:38,712 --> 00:04:41,260
and changing one byte changes all of them.

49
00:04:41,968 --> 00:04:46,441
The file itself is not in the database. It sits in private object storage,

50
00:04:46,441 --> 00:04:52,426
called RustFS here and Cloudflare R2 in production, under a server-made key that includes the hash.

51
00:04:52,426 --> 00:04:58,048
This demo shares one bucket with the development setup, but this document has its own object.

52
00:04:58,761 --> 00:05:03,461
The uploader then submits the document for review, and the status becomes Submitted.

53
00:05:04,169 --> 00:05:10,559
In the app, Version History shows the fingerprint, shortened. The full value is in the database.

54
00:05:11,276 --> 00:05:14,381
Now the negative cases. The same user tries again,

55
00:05:14,381 --> 00:05:19,226
but the browser reports a place about one hundred kilometres outside the zone.

56
00:05:19,935 --> 00:05:26,242
The server answers GEOFENCE_DENIED, and nothing is stored. The server decides inside or outside,

57
00:05:26,242 --> 00:05:27,425
never the browser.

58
00:05:28,139 --> 00:05:34,099
Now the location is inside the zone, but very imprecise: plus or minus five hundred metres.

59
00:05:34,805 --> 00:05:40,335
The server refuses that too, with LOCATION_LOW_CONFIDENCE. This is called fail-closed:

60
00:05:40,335 --> 00:05:46,765
if the system is not sure, it says no. This server allows up to three hundred metres of uncertainty.

61
00:05:47,479 --> 00:05:52,583
The zone denial is written to the audit log with the claimed position, as you can see here.

62
00:05:52,583 --> 00:05:58,752
The low-confidence refusal is different: it happens before the zone check, and it goes to the application log,

63
00:05:58,752 --> 00:05:59,929
not to the audit log.

64
00:06:00,630 --> 00:06:05,798
An honest limit: browser location can be faked, for example with developer tools.

65
00:06:05,798 --> 00:06:12,178
So geofencing here is a policy control that adds a layer. It is not a guarantee of where someone is.

66
00:06:12,178 --> 00:06:16,580
The tamper-evidence comes from the blockchain, not from the geofence.

67
00:06:17,288 --> 00:06:20,389
Next, review and approval. This uses maker-checker,

68
00:06:20,389 --> 00:06:24,828
meaning the person who made a document must not be the one who checks it.

69
00:06:25,532 --> 00:06:31,266
Here a Legal Officer opens a seeded document that they uploaded themselves. There is no Approve button,

70
00:06:31,266 --> 00:06:33,882
and the page says someone else must approve it.

71
00:06:34,583 --> 00:06:41,089
If they call the server directly anyway, from inside the zone, the answer is MAKER_CHECKER_VIOLATION.

72
00:06:41,089 --> 00:06:45,983
That is a real response, shown in a supplementary view. Nothing was changed.

73
00:06:46,691 --> 00:06:52,556
Now the real path for our lease. A Reviewing Officer, a different person from the uploader,

74
00:06:52,556 --> 00:06:54,361
opens it and chooses Review.

75
00:06:55,071 --> 00:07:01,437
They pass the review, and the status becomes Pending Approval. A reviewer can instead request changes,

76
00:07:01,437 --> 00:07:05,681
which sends it back to the uploader. This app has no rejected state.

77
00:07:06,389 --> 00:07:12,020
Then a Legal Officer who did not upload it opens the document. The Approve button is there.

78
00:07:12,020 --> 00:07:14,309
The server checks the location first.

79
00:07:15,012 --> 00:07:19,240
Approval starts anchoring automatically, and there is no anchor button.

80
00:07:19,240 --> 00:07:22,991
The backend's own wallet signs the transaction, never the user.

81
00:07:22,991 --> 00:07:28,350
This is a real transaction on the Sepolia test network, the free test version of Ethereum.

82
00:07:28,350 --> 00:07:30,612
I have trimmed the wait for the block.

83
00:07:32,350 --> 00:07:38,950
The status becomes Active. The approval cost a tiny amount of test Ether, which has no real value.

84
00:07:39,660 --> 00:07:46,944
Now, what exactly went on the blockchain. In Version History, version one is Active and marked Confirmed.

85
00:07:46,944 --> 00:07:48,470
We open View on-chain.

86
00:07:49,210 --> 00:07:55,820
The Blockchain Anchor Record puts two hashes side by side. On the left, the hash recorded in our database.

87
00:07:55,820 --> 00:08:02,743
On the right, the hash the app has just read live from the contract on the Sepolia testnet. They are identical,

88
00:08:02,743 --> 00:08:04,240
and the page says Match.

89
00:08:04,940 --> 00:08:11,879
The page also shows the network label, Sepolia testnet, the contract address, and the transaction hash,

90
00:08:11,879 --> 00:08:13,160
with a Copy button.

91
00:08:13,890 --> 00:08:20,887
The smart contract, DocumentAnchor, stores only four things per document version: the hash, the version number,

92
00:08:20,887 --> 00:08:26,939
an event type, and the block time. The PDF, its filename and any person's name are not on-chain,

93
00:08:26,939 --> 00:08:33,810
because a blockchain is public and permanent. The contract also refuses a second anchor for the same version.

94
00:08:34,540 --> 00:08:41,780
On the public Etherscan page for the Sepolia testnet, the transaction shows Status: Success, and a tiny fee.

95
00:08:42,540 --> 00:08:49,174
One service wallet in the backend signs every anchor. That is a trust assumption we come back to at the end.

96
00:08:49,174 --> 00:08:52,430
And there is no Merkle tree: each file gets one hash.

97
00:08:53,190 --> 00:08:59,460
Now clearance. The throwaway user has the lowest level, Public, and our lease is Internal.

98
00:09:00,190 --> 00:09:05,720
In the repository the lease is simply not listed, because the server filters in the database query.

99
00:09:06,490 --> 00:09:11,037
Opening its exact link shows the app's own wording: That item couldn't be found,

100
00:09:11,037 --> 00:09:12,800
or you don't have access to it.

101
00:09:13,500 --> 00:09:21,224
In the supplementary view, the real document and a made-up id give exactly the same answer, 404 not found.

102
00:09:21,224 --> 00:09:28,730
We use 404 instead of 403, which means forbidden, because a 403 would confirm that the document exists.

103
00:09:29,450 --> 00:09:35,110
The same rule applies to everyone. Here the Administrator, whose clearance is Internal,

104
00:09:35,110 --> 00:09:42,137
opens a seeded document classified Confidential, and also gets a real 404. Administrators manage the system,

105
00:09:42,137 --> 00:09:45,260
but get no automatic access to document content.

106
00:09:45,960 --> 00:09:52,632
A refused attempt on a document that exists is still written to the audit log as ACCESS_DENIED, as highlighted.

107
00:09:52,632 --> 00:09:56,720
This is access control inside the application. It is not encryption.

108
00:09:57,460 --> 00:10:04,060
Now the core feature: verification. The Legal Officer opens Integrity Verification for version one.

109
00:10:04,760 --> 00:10:09,220
When we run it, the server reads the stored file and recomputes its SHA-256.

110
00:10:09,220 --> 00:10:12,917
It compares that with the hash saved in the database at upload,

111
00:10:12,917 --> 00:10:16,850
and with the anchored hash it reads live from the Sepolia contract.

112
00:10:17,560 --> 00:10:21,040
All three are identical, so the result is VERIFIED.

113
00:10:21,760 --> 00:10:27,071
A database change alone, or a storage change alone, cannot produce a false VERIFIED,

114
00:10:27,071 --> 00:10:29,790
because the chain holds the original value.

115
00:10:30,490 --> 00:10:35,915
Now the attack. First, for the demo, I save a copy of the original file bytes locally,

116
00:10:35,915 --> 00:10:42,160
and check that their hash equals the recorded fingerprint. That lets me put things back afterwards.

117
00:10:42,890 --> 00:10:48,757
Now someone with direct access to the storage server changes one byte of the file, not through the app.

118
00:10:48,757 --> 00:10:54,225
This script bypasses the application on purpose, and it touches only this document's own object.

119
00:10:54,225 --> 00:10:57,700
The database and the blockchain still hold the original hash.

120
00:10:58,400 --> 00:11:02,651
We run verification again. The result is MISMATCH, tamper detected.

121
00:11:02,651 --> 00:11:08,616
The recomputed hash is completely different, while the stored and on-chain hashes still agree.

122
00:11:08,616 --> 00:11:14,010
One changed byte gives a totally different hash. That is called the avalanche effect.

123
00:11:14,710 --> 00:11:21,400
The document is also flagged as Tampered. The system made the change evident. It did not prevent it.

124
00:11:22,110 --> 00:11:25,863
Now I write the original bytes back to the same storage key,

125
00:11:25,863 --> 00:11:29,430
and confirm that their hash matches the recorded SHA-256.

126
00:11:30,130 --> 00:11:32,630
Verification returns to VERIFIED.

127
00:11:33,330 --> 00:11:38,960
But the Tampered flag is still on the document. A later pass does not clear it automatically.

128
00:11:39,730 --> 00:11:43,699
Only an Administrator can clear it. They choose Clear integrity flag,

129
00:11:43,699 --> 00:11:48,590
and must give a reason of at least ten characters, which is written to the audit log.

130
00:11:49,290 --> 00:11:56,029
The app re-runs verification on every anchored version first, and only then clears the flag. The flag is gone,

131
00:11:56,029 --> 00:11:57,990
and the document is clean again.

132
00:11:58,690 --> 00:12:05,867
Now an amendment, which creates version two. Documents are never edited in place. Each version is a new,

133
00:12:05,867 --> 00:12:07,040
immutable record.

134
00:12:34,540 --> 00:12:40,118
A Legal Officer opens the active lease and chooses Request amendment. This is geofenced,

135
00:12:40,118 --> 00:12:41,830
so the location is checked.

136
00:12:42,540 --> 00:12:46,960
They must give a reason. The document moves to Amendment Requested.

137
00:12:47,690 --> 00:12:52,153
Now Authorized Staff uploads the amended file, version two. The title,

138
00:12:52,153 --> 00:12:56,680
type and classification are copied from the document and cannot change.

139
00:12:57,390 --> 00:13:01,210
Version two starts as a Draft, and its uploader submits it.

140
00:13:01,910 --> 00:13:06,910
The three-person rule applies again. The Reviewing Officer reviews version two.

141
00:13:07,610 --> 00:13:11,336
And the Legal Officer, who did not upload it, approves.

142
00:13:11,336 --> 00:13:18,450
This sends a second real transaction on the Sepolia test network, for version two. Again I trim the wait.

143
00:13:20,810 --> 00:13:27,368
In Version History, version two is Active and Confirmed. Version one now carries the grey Superseded badge.

144
00:13:27,368 --> 00:13:31,964
That is the only place the app says so. There is no separate current label:

145
00:13:31,964 --> 00:13:34,600
the Active badge marks the current version.

146
00:13:35,310 --> 00:13:40,618
The hashes are shortened on that page, so this supplementary view shows the proof.

147
00:13:40,618 --> 00:13:47,028
The previous-hash field of version two equals, character for character, the SHA-256 of version one.

148
00:13:47,028 --> 00:13:51,560
There is no parent version id field. This hash link is the connection.

149
00:13:52,260 --> 00:13:58,150
Version two has its own anchor, again with the recorded and on-chain hashes side by side, and a Match.

150
00:13:58,910 --> 00:14:01,970
Verification of version two returns VERIFIED.

151
00:14:02,710 --> 00:14:08,238
And version one still verifies, because its file, its hash and its anchor are untouched.

152
00:14:08,238 --> 00:14:11,820
The Verification page does not mention superseded at all.

153
00:14:12,560 --> 00:14:19,859
Now download. The Download button gives the current version only, which is now version two. It is geofenced,

154
00:14:19,859 --> 00:14:22,090
so the location is checked first.

155
00:14:22,810 --> 00:14:27,727
The server answers with a short-lived pre-signed link, valid for sixty seconds.

156
00:14:27,727 --> 00:14:32,770
The file never passes through the API. I will not show the link itself on screen.

157
00:14:33,470 --> 00:14:39,483
To prove the download is genuine, the test harness fetched the file through that link and hashed it.

158
00:14:39,483 --> 00:14:42,730
The result equals the recorded SHA-256 of version two.

159
00:14:43,470 --> 00:14:48,250
The download is audited as ACCESS, with the version id, as highlighted.

160
00:14:48,970 --> 00:14:55,720
Now the real denials. From outside the zone, the same Download button is refused with GEOFENCE_DENIED.

161
00:14:56,470 --> 00:15:02,050
And a below-clearance viewer who is inside an assigned zone gets a 404 for the same document,

162
00:15:02,050 --> 00:15:07,390
so the file is never even offered. The order of checks is the zone first, then clearance.

163
00:15:08,120 --> 00:15:15,130
Finally, the end of the lifecycle: archive. The lease is Active, and it is in the repository list.

164
00:15:15,870 --> 00:15:22,180
The Legal Officer opens it and chooses Archive. There is no reason field, and it is not geofenced.

165
00:15:22,920 --> 00:15:25,020
The status is now Archived.

166
00:15:25,720 --> 00:15:29,240
It has disappeared from the default Dashboard and Repository.

167
00:15:32,220 --> 00:15:35,530
It appears when we filter the Repository on Archived.

168
00:15:36,230 --> 00:15:39,689
Archiving keeps every version, anchor and file untouched.

169
00:15:39,689 --> 00:15:45,940
The Reports panel will count one more archived document. And the backend has no way to undo an archive:

170
00:15:45,940 --> 00:15:50,310
there is no restore route. Only direct database editing could change it.

171
00:15:51,080 --> 00:15:58,000
There are six possible verification results. One more, live: a seeded draft that was never approved.

172
00:15:58,700 --> 00:16:04,947
The result is NOT ANCHORED. The file is intact, but there is no blockchain record to compare with.

173
00:16:04,947 --> 00:16:07,370
That is not an error and not an alarm.

174
00:16:08,100 --> 00:16:10,270
Here are all six outcomes.

175
00:16:10,970 --> 00:16:16,793
VERIFIED: the stored file, the recorded hash and the on-chain hash all agree. MISMATCH:

176
00:16:16,793 --> 00:16:19,940
the file differs from the record, which we saw.

177
00:16:20,640 --> 00:16:26,640
NOT_ANCHORED: it was never approved, so there is nothing on-chain to compare, which we just saw.

178
00:16:27,340 --> 00:16:32,946
ANCHOR_MISSING: the database says it was anchored, but the chain says no. FILE_MISSING:

179
00:16:32,946 --> 00:16:38,166
the stored file is gone. And CHAIN_UNREACHABLE: the blockchain could not be read,

180
00:16:38,166 --> 00:16:40,100
so there is no verdict at all.

181
00:16:40,800 --> 00:16:46,200
I did not trigger those last three live. They are marked on the slide as not triggered live.

182
00:16:46,900 --> 00:16:53,290
Login also has protection. On the throwaway account only, I type wrong passwords on purpose.

183
00:16:53,990 --> 00:16:57,890
The page counts the attempts. Attempt one of five.

184
00:16:58,640 --> 00:17:00,000
Attempt two.

185
00:17:00,740 --> 00:17:02,170
Attempt three.

186
00:17:02,890 --> 00:17:04,330
Attempt four.

187
00:17:05,030 --> 00:17:09,130
Attempt five. Sign-in is now paused for about a minute.

188
00:17:09,880 --> 00:17:16,000
The next try is refused outright: too many attempts. That slows down password guessing.

189
00:17:16,700 --> 00:17:22,990
In MongoDB, the login_rate_limits collection holds the counter for each email, and when the pause ends.

190
00:17:23,690 --> 00:17:30,934
Finally, where everything lives. The Auditor opens Audit Logs, and it opens at the top, newest first.

191
00:17:30,934 --> 00:17:36,960
Every action is a row: who, what, to what, and the result. Rows are only ever added.

192
00:17:37,660 --> 00:17:43,140
The Action dropdown lists every action the log really contains. Here I pick Approve.

193
00:17:43,860 --> 00:17:48,660
Actor search finds people by name or email. Here, Legal.

194
00:17:49,360 --> 00:17:55,880
The document filter finds a document by its title, only among documents this person may open. Here,

195
00:17:55,880 --> 00:17:58,580
a seeded board resolution, and its trail.

196
00:17:59,280 --> 00:18:04,560
For our lease, the same view is one click from the document page: View audit trail.

197
00:18:05,280 --> 00:18:11,530
This is the whole life of the lease, with names instead of ids. The ids are secondary text underneath.

198
00:18:13,080 --> 00:18:20,208
Next, the MongoDB collections. Users, geofences, documents and document versions are the authoritative data.

199
00:18:20,208 --> 00:18:25,092
The anchors collection is derived, because the chain is the real evidence.

200
00:18:25,092 --> 00:18:28,260
Verification records and audit logs are history.

201
00:18:28,980 --> 00:18:33,949
The ids link them: a version points to its document, an anchor points to its version,

202
00:18:33,949 --> 00:18:36,230
and the storage key points to the file.

203
00:18:36,930 --> 00:18:43,887
And the split: MongoDB holds state, fingerprints and audit. The private bucket holds the file bytes.

204
00:18:43,887 --> 00:18:48,480
The Sepolia contract holds the hash, version, event type and time.

205
00:18:49,180 --> 00:18:55,805
One honest gap: the audit log has no hash chain yet, so someone with database write access could alter it.

206
00:18:55,805 --> 00:18:57,680
That is a planned improvement.

207
00:18:58,430 --> 00:19:02,980
The Reports panel gives the whole picture. The Auditor can open it too.

208
00:19:03,680 --> 00:19:08,150
Anchors confirmed counts the successful transactions, including our two.

209
00:19:08,880 --> 00:19:14,363
This note reconciles the counts. The Auditor can access twenty-two documents in total,

210
00:19:14,363 --> 00:19:18,762
including three archived. The Dashboard and Repository show nineteen,

211
00:19:18,762 --> 00:19:21,440
because they leave archived documents out.

